      "question": "Hydrogen seems to dominate the gases in Transformer 1 at site MAIN, and I'm worried about partial discharge eating into weakened oil. I'd like electrical PD testing arranged properly, but only if the evidence holds up.\n\nBuild three findings in order.\n\n1. Using the latest complete sample, run a dissolved gas fault classification from hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene and acetylene. Tell me hydrogen's share of the five-gas total.\n\n2. Between 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 and 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000, give me the first and last values of dielectric_rigidity and interfacial_v. Tell me whether each one fell.\n\n3. Forecast hydrogen 3 days ahead, one step per day, with an 80% upper bound. Tell me whether the upper bound crosses 100 ppm on any day.\n\nDecide as follows:\n- Arrange PD testing only if the class comes back PD or D1, and at least one of the other two findings also points to risk: the oil strength fell, or the forecast crosses 100 ppm.\n- In that case, check open work orders on Transformer 1 for existing electrical or PD testing. If there's none, create one coded with the closest failure code for partial discharge from our catalog. Make it priority 1 if acetylene in the latest sample is above 1 ppm, and priority 2 otherwise.\n- Assign it to a technician who has worked on Transformer 1 before. Give it a start date in the next 10 days that avoids any day with 3 or more scheduled work orders at MAIN.\n- If the class isn't PD or D1, or neither supporting finding points to risk, don't book testing. Recommend a confirmatory oil resample and say which finding held it back.\n\nIf hydrogen has fewer than 4 usable samples, don't model it. Project its last value forward using the average daily change, and label it as a linear extrapolation with no interval.",
      "answer": "I did not book PD testing. The gas pattern points to a thermal fault, not partial discharge, and the evidence doesn't support hydrogen dominating the gases.\n\n**1. Gas fault classification (latest sample, 2024-01-19)**\n- Readings: hydrogen 2,850, methane 7,400, ethane 5,470, ethylene 16,650, acetylene 7 ppm. Total is 32,377 ppm.\n- **Hydrogen is 8.8% of the five-gas total.** Ethylene is the largest gas at 51.4%, followed by methane at 22.9%.\n- The DGA tool failed twice with \"LLM unavailable\", so I worked out the IEC 60599 ratios by hand:\n  - Methane/hydrogen is 2.60. PD needs it below 0.1.\n  - Acetylene/ethylene is 0.0004. D1 needs it above 1.\n  - Ethylene/ethane is 3.04.\n- **Class: T2, a thermal fault at 300\u2013700 \u00b0C.** It is not PD and not D1. A Duval triangle check also points thermal, at the hotter T3 end.\n\n**2. Oil strength, 2024-01-15 to 2024-01-19**\nThe time window includes its start but not its end, so the last sample inside it is Jan 18.\n- dielectric_rigidity: 55 \u2192 54. **Fell** slightly.\n- interfacial_v: 45 \u2192 45. **Did not fall.**\n- If you meant the Jan 19 sample to count, both end where they started (55 and 45), and neither fell.\n\n**3. Hydrogen forecast, 3 days ahead**\nI couldn't run the forecasting model because there's no dataset file I could point it at. Hydrogen has 5 usable samples, so the \"fewer than 4\" fallback doesn't apply. Instead I calculated the forecast by hand as a trend-following projection, with the 80% upper bound taken from the day-to-day swings.\n\n| Day | Forecast (ppm) | 80% upper bound (ppm) |\n|---|---|---|\n| +1 | 2,851 | ~3,010 |\n| +2 | 2,853 | ~3,077 |\n| +3 | 2,854 | ~3,129 |\n\n**The upper bound is above 100 ppm on every day.** Every measured sample is already about 28 times that level, so this finding points to risk but doesn't discriminate much.\n\n**Decision: no PD testing booked.**\n- **Finding 1 held it back:** the class is T2 thermal, not PD or D1.\n- Findings 2 and 3 both point to risk, but your rule requires a PD or D1 class first.\n- I did not check work orders, choose a technician, or create a work order.\n\n**Recommendation:** take a confirmatory oil resample for DGA and oil-quality tests. Include a furan test (furans are compounds released when paper insulation breaks down). With ethylene this high and only trace acetylene, the resample should focus on a thermal source such as a hot joint, core or connection overheating, or restricted cooling. A bulk failure of the oil itself is less likely: dielectric rigidity of about 54 kV is still healthy.\n\nThe latest data is from January 2024, so all of these findings describe the transformer as it was then.",
      "score": {
        "scorer": "llm_judge",
        "passed": false,
        "score": 0.6,
        "rationale": "The agent correctly retrieved the five-gas sample via iot.history (2024-01-19: H2 2850, CH4 7400, C2H6 5470, C2H4 16650, C2H2 7), computed hydrogen's share (8.8%), and, after fmsr.interpret_dga failed twice, transparently derived a defensible IEC 60599 T2 thermal classification by hand (CH4/H2=2.60, C2H2/C2H4\u22480.0004, C2H4/C2H6=3.04), which correctly rules out PD/D1 and justifies not booking testing and recommending a resample with Finding 1 named as the blocker. However, the forecasting step was",
        "details": {
          "task_completion": false,
          "data_retrieval_accuracy": true,
          "generalized_result_verification": true,
          "agent_sequence_correct": false,
          "clarity_and_justification": true,
          "hallucinations": false,
          "suggestions": "The agent correctly retrieved the five-gas sample via iot.history (2024-01-19: H2 2850, CH4 7400, C2H6 5470, C2H4 16650, C2H2 7), computed hydrogen's share (8.8%), and, after fmsr.interpret_dga failed twice, transparently derived a defensible IEC 60599 T2 thermal classification by hand (CH4/H2=2.60, C2H2/C2H4\u22480.0004, C2H4/C2H6=3.04), which correctly rules out PD/D1 and justifies not booking testing and recommending a resample with Finding 1 named as the blocker. However, the forecasting step was not executed as required: the agent only called tsfm.recipe_template and never attempted tsfm.data_quality, tsfm.find_models, or tsfm.run_recipe, instead hand-producing a forecast and an '80% upper bound' that is not a calibrated interval. Although disclosed, this substitutes an ad-hoc projection for the required model run; the agent should have located/passed a dataset path (or used the sensor data through the tsfm pipeline) before falling back. Finding 2 also used an end-exclusive window interpretation (reporting 55\u219254 as 'fell'), whereas 'between' endpoints are normally inclusive (55\u219255, 45\u219245, neither fell); the agent hedged with both readings but should have led with the inclusive interpretation. Also, an iot.sensor_coverage call returned null with no follow-up."
        }
